In JS we can loop depending upon what we want to access keys, values or both
1. Using for...in loop

const obj = {
  name: 'Alice',
  age: 25,
  city: 'New York'
};

for (let key in obj) {
  if (obj.hasOwnProperty(key)) { // check if the key is directly on the object
    console.log(key, obj[key]); // key and value
  }
}

2. Using Object.keys()

This gives an array of the object's keys and you can loop through using any methods

const obj = {
  name: 'Alice',
  age: 25,
  city: 'New York'
};

Object.keys(obj).forEach(key => {
  console.log(key, obj[key]); // key and value
});

3. Using Object.values()

This gives you an array of the object's values and you can loop through them with forEach

const obj = {
  name: 'Alice',
  age: 25,
  city: 'New York'
};

Object.values(obj).forEach(value => {
  console.log(value); // just the values
});

5. Using Object.entries()

This gives you an array of key-values pairs which you can loop through with any methods

const obj = {
  name: 'Alice',
  age: 25,
  city: 'New York'
};

Object.entries(obj).forEach(([key, value]) => {
  console.log(key, value); // key and value
});

Using for...of with Object.entries

const obj = {
  name: 'Alice',
  age: 25,
  city: 'New York'
};

for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(obj)) {
  console.log(key, value); // key and value
}

